{"text": "Children will engage with materials more purposefully, and for longer, when there is some ambiguity involved, and thus, something they are interested in figuring out. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In studies of toddlers and preschoolers, researchers have found that children spontaneously engage in more exploratory play, and use more strategies for testing evidence, with a toy designed to be difficult to figure out. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In one study, young children explored a lightbox that appeared to turn on and off when different blocks were placed on it. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In one condition, the same block always turned the box on or off. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In another, inconsistent results were included, for example, a block that had previously turned the box on did not the next time. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> When faced with inconsistent and difficult to interpret results, children spent more time exploring the lightbox and were more likely to provide causal explanations trying to understand why the objects did and did not turn on the lightbox. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Further, researchers found that when children proposed causal explanations, they tried out more strategies for testing their ideas.", "alignment": {"characters": ["C", "h", "i", "l", "d", "r", "e", "n", " ", "w", "i", "l", "l", " ", "e", "n", "g", "a", "g", "e", " ", "w", "i", "t", "h", " ", "m", "a", "t", "e", "r", "i", "a", "l", "s", " ", "m", "o", "r", "e", " ", "p", "u", "r", "p", "o", "s", "e", "f", "u", "l", "l", "y", ",", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "f", "o", "r", " ", "l", "o", "n", "g", "e", "r", ",", " ", "w", "h", "e", "n", " ", "t", "h", "e", "r", "e", " ", "i", "s", " ", "s", "o", "m", "e", " ", "a", "m", "b", "i", "g", "u", "i", "t", "y", " ", "i", "n", "v", "o", "l", "v", "e", "d", ",", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "t", "h", "u", "s", ",", " ", "s", "o", "m", "e", "t", "h", "i", "n", "g", " ", "t", "h", "e", "y", " ", "a", "r", "e", " ", "i", "n", "t", "e", "r", "e", "s", "t", 